WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:11.370
&gt;&gt; Hi, my name is Susan Dumais.

00:00:11.371 --> 00:00:12.750
I'm the Assistant Director of

00:00:12.751 --> 00:00:15.100
Microsoft's Research AI lab.

00:00:15.101 --> 00:00:17.210
I'm here today with
Michael Jordan,

00:00:17.211 --> 00:00:20.340
who is one of the world
renowned computer scientists

00:00:20.341 --> 00:00:22.065
and statisticians in the world.

00:00:22.066 --> 00:00:23.970
He's been at Microsoft earlier

00:00:23.971 --> 00:00:26.460
today as part of
our faculty summit,

00:00:26.461 --> 00:00:28.535
whose topic this year is

00:00:28.536 --> 00:00:30.870
systems and the future
disruptions,

00:00:30.871 --> 00:00:33.105
that will be enabled by
progress and systems.

00:00:33.106 --> 00:00:35.880
One of the topics obviously
in thinking about

00:00:35.881 --> 00:00:39.220
the next generation of systems
is where AI meets systems.

00:00:39.221 --> 00:00:42.270
So, there have been
sessions on AI for systems,

00:00:42.271 --> 00:00:43.440
and systems for AI.

00:00:43.441 --> 00:00:45.855
Mike has been incredibly

00:00:45.856 --> 00:00:48.080
involved in some of
those discussions.

00:00:48.081 --> 00:00:53.340
So, Michael is a Pehong Chen
Distinguished Professor

00:00:53.341 --> 00:00:55.995
in EECS at Berkeley.

00:00:55.996 --> 00:00:57.960
He's also on the faculty in

00:00:57.961 --> 00:01:00.660
the Statistics
Department and part of

00:01:00.661 --> 00:01:04.060
a couple of really interesting
multidisciplinary labs.

00:01:04.061 --> 00:01:07.140
One on algorithms
machines and people,

00:01:07.141 --> 00:01:09.770
and the other on BAIR's,

00:01:09.771 --> 00:01:12.980
Berkeley AI Research or
something like that.

00:01:12.981 --> 00:01:18.350
Michael has a background
in psychology,

00:01:18.351 --> 00:01:20.930
statistics, and
cognitive science.

00:01:20.931 --> 00:01:24.600
So, he brings a really broad
perspective to problems.

00:01:24.601 --> 00:01:26.675
One of the things that I
think is most impressive,

00:01:26.676 --> 00:01:29.230
Mike, about your background is,

00:01:29.231 --> 00:01:32.700
that you've won a tremendous
number of publications,

00:01:32.701 --> 00:01:35.370
tremendous influence in the areas

00:01:35.371 --> 00:01:37.280
of computer science,
and statistics.

00:01:37.281 --> 00:01:39.870
But your awards come
from a broad set

00:01:39.871 --> 00:01:42.900
of organizations and fields.

00:01:42.901 --> 00:01:45.575
So, clearly, you're
a fellow of the ACM,

00:01:45.576 --> 00:01:47.040
but you're also a fellow of

00:01:47.041 --> 00:01:49.400
the American Statistical
Association,

00:01:49.401 --> 00:01:53.255
the Cognitive Science Society,
the Bayesian Society.

00:01:53.256 --> 00:01:57.015
I want to start
the discussion today

00:01:57.016 --> 00:02:00.870
by having you reflect a little
bit on your graduate work.

00:02:00.871 --> 00:02:03.340
You were at UCSD
with Dave Rumelhart,

00:02:03.341 --> 00:02:05.510
and others in the mid-80s,

00:02:05.511 --> 00:02:08.170
at maybe the second
renaissance of

00:02:08.171 --> 00:02:10.935
the Artificial
Intelligence's area,

00:02:10.936 --> 00:02:14.370
working in parallel and
distributed systems.

00:02:14.371 --> 00:02:16.830
I still have my two volumes
of the PDP books with

00:02:16.831 --> 00:02:20.085
the three-and-a-half inch
floppy disks in the back.

00:02:20.086 --> 00:02:21.720
Can you say a little bit about,

00:02:21.721 --> 00:02:24.410
maybe the intellectual
environment at the time?

00:02:24.411 --> 00:02:26.240
This field was really nascent,

00:02:26.241 --> 00:02:28.590
as well as how it's
influenced some of

00:02:28.591 --> 00:02:30.480
your subsequent thinking about

00:02:30.481 --> 00:02:33.320
problems of intelligence
and computation?

00:02:33.321 --> 00:02:35.775
&gt;&gt; Sure. First of all it's
a pleasure to be here.

00:02:35.776 --> 00:02:37.955
I've known Susan for
a number of years.

00:02:37.956 --> 00:02:39.930
I've been given lots of

00:02:39.931 --> 00:02:41.110
interviews in
the last few years as

00:02:41.111 --> 00:02:42.370
the interest in
the fields picked up.

00:02:42.371 --> 00:02:43.950
It's the first time
I've given interview

00:02:43.951 --> 00:02:45.630
to someone who probably knows
more than I know about,

00:02:45.631 --> 00:02:47.500
half of the topics we're
going to be talking about,

00:02:47.501 --> 00:02:49.590
and usually you have to
be explaining things.

00:02:49.591 --> 00:02:51.330
Yes.

00:02:51.331 --> 00:02:53.265
So, I've moved around a lot in

00:02:53.266 --> 00:02:56.010
my life and it's not directed,

00:02:56.011 --> 00:02:57.665
it's partly random walk.

00:02:57.666 --> 00:02:59.285
It's just because of curiosity,

00:02:59.286 --> 00:03:01.760
and also a feeling that
not any one person,

00:03:01.761 --> 00:03:03.505
or any one field has
got all the answers.

00:03:03.506 --> 00:03:05.260
It's by synthesizing

00:03:05.261 --> 00:03:07.895
various people's
perspectives and ideas.

00:03:07.896 --> 00:03:09.660
I was thinking
about this earlier.

00:03:09.661 --> 00:03:10.570
How I might explain that?

00:03:10.571 --> 00:03:12.440
So, one of my friends, Yana Khan,

00:03:12.441 --> 00:03:15.000
refers to me as the Miles Davis
of machine learning,

00:03:15.001 --> 00:03:16.430
not the Michael Jordan,

00:03:16.431 --> 00:03:17.700
because he was an example,

00:03:17.701 --> 00:03:19.050
someone who moved throughout

00:03:19.051 --> 00:03:20.945
his career just try new things.

00:03:20.946 --> 00:03:23.410
Another anecdote was
when I was a kid,

00:03:23.411 --> 00:03:26.540
my grandparents took me to
Europe for the first time.

00:03:26.541 --> 00:03:29.760
We went to Sweden,
and I loved it.

00:03:29.761 --> 00:03:31.820
At some point apparently,

00:03:31.821 --> 00:03:34.035
I was lost, They said
they thought I was lost.

00:03:34.036 --> 00:03:35.280
They couldn't find me for half a

00:03:35.281 --> 00:03:36.585
day and finally they found me.

00:03:36.586 --> 00:03:38.090
I was with a bunch
of Swedish children

00:03:38.091 --> 00:03:39.780
because I've decided
I'd become Swedish.

00:03:39.781 --> 00:03:41.460
Because it just that, I should

00:03:41.461 --> 00:03:42.730
just do one thing in my life,

00:03:42.731 --> 00:03:44.130
I should become a Swedish child.

00:03:44.131 --> 00:03:49.080
So, I think that I was,

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:53.160
first of all, I had
a bad American education.

00:03:53.161 --> 00:03:56.875
So, I was lucky that I arrived
at age 20, I knew nothing.

00:03:56.876 --> 00:03:59.490
So, I could sort of met
my own ideas about what to work

00:03:59.491 --> 00:04:02.325
on and I had,

00:04:02.326 --> 00:04:05.100
I think like most of that age,

00:04:05.101 --> 00:04:07.030
read some philosophy and
found that interesting,

00:04:07.031 --> 00:04:08.520
and I was lucky enough
to be in an era

00:04:08.521 --> 00:04:11.060
where there had been
analytic philosophy.

00:04:11.061 --> 00:04:12.510
So Bertrand Russell had written

00:04:12.511 --> 00:04:16.050
an autobiography, and
very presciently,

00:04:16.051 --> 00:04:17.355
he was talking about

00:04:17.356 --> 00:04:20.100
the mind and the
scientific perspective on

00:04:20.101 --> 00:04:22.440
it and how logic

00:04:22.441 --> 00:04:24.230
and mathematics and all
arose out of the mind,

00:04:24.231 --> 00:04:26.190
and just felt right
to me that there

00:04:26.191 --> 00:04:28.490
was something deeper about that.

00:04:28.491 --> 00:04:30.750
He had it with a sense of

00:04:30.751 --> 00:04:31.790
humor and a feeling that there's

00:04:31.791 --> 00:04:33.350
something new going to
happen in this area.

00:04:33.351 --> 00:04:36.295
So, that helped to influence me.

00:04:36.296 --> 00:04:38.660
Then just by hooking what
was available to me,

00:04:38.661 --> 00:04:40.350
I was a programmer at San Diego.

00:04:40.351 --> 00:04:43.050
It was largely because

00:04:43.051 --> 00:04:44.280
of experimental psychology that

00:04:44.281 --> 00:04:45.690
I found my first interest.

00:04:45.691 --> 00:04:47.790
I remember a work by people
like Roger Shepherd,

00:04:47.791 --> 00:04:49.420
where you talk about
mental rotation,

00:04:49.421 --> 00:04:50.700
and just the idea you could

00:04:50.701 --> 00:04:52.240
probe into a system
that was very,

00:04:52.241 --> 00:04:53.580
very complicated and still gleam

00:04:53.581 --> 00:04:55.640
things about it from the outside.

00:04:55.641 --> 00:04:57.330
It was fascinating.

00:04:57.331 --> 00:04:58.690
I was just impressed
by the skills

00:04:58.691 --> 00:05:00.455
that people to be
able to do that.

00:05:00.456 --> 00:05:02.380
I think psychology
though was just so,

00:05:02.381 --> 00:05:06.020
so hard, where probing into
a massively complex system.

00:05:06.021 --> 00:05:07.030
There was nothing else like it.

00:05:07.031 --> 00:05:09.360
Even think about the economic
system of New York City.

00:05:09.361 --> 00:05:10.925
It's nothing like
close to the brain,

00:05:10.926 --> 00:05:13.560
and think about probing
that from the from

00:05:13.561 --> 00:05:14.880
the atmosphere while
you're looking at

00:05:14.881 --> 00:05:16.890
the lights or the emissions.

00:05:16.891 --> 00:05:17.940
It's just where we're at.

00:05:17.941 --> 00:05:20.580
So, I still feel

00:05:20.581 --> 00:05:22.560
that that is the
the field of the future,

00:05:22.561 --> 00:05:24.420
of the next several
hundred years.

00:05:24.421 --> 00:05:25.890
I think it'll take that long to

00:05:25.891 --> 00:05:27.615
start to really get
insights into it.

00:05:27.616 --> 00:05:29.980
Since I have unfortunately
short lifetime,

00:05:29.981 --> 00:05:34.270
I thought I'd better move
on to faster moving fields.

00:05:34.271 --> 00:05:36.930
I think kind of luckily
is part of that,

00:05:36.931 --> 00:05:38.820
I'd also studied some statistics

00:05:38.821 --> 00:05:40.220
because you had to
analyze your data.

00:05:40.221 --> 00:05:42.330
I started to appreciate
not just the use of

00:05:42.331 --> 00:05:43.550
statistics and calculating

00:05:43.551 --> 00:05:44.730
P values and
an analysis variance,

00:05:44.731 --> 00:05:47.700
but the 300-year-old tradition of

00:05:47.701 --> 00:05:50.980
inferential thinking that
was embodied in the ideas,

00:05:50.981 --> 00:05:52.320
and where were with
those principles

00:05:52.321 --> 00:05:53.500
coming from and
what do they mean,

00:05:53.501 --> 00:05:54.990
and surely that has
something to do with

00:05:54.991 --> 00:05:56.520
how humans approach the world.

00:05:56.521 --> 00:05:57.715
So, I was already

00:05:57.716 --> 00:05:58.920
thinking that what's
most interesting about

00:05:58.921 --> 00:06:01.410
humans is their ability

00:06:01.411 --> 00:06:02.980
to abstract something
about the world,

00:06:02.981 --> 00:06:05.100
and then their ability to deal
with all the uncertainty,

00:06:05.101 --> 00:06:07.050
and to make plans and
that sort of thing.

00:06:07.051 --> 00:06:10.020
So, I was less interested in

00:06:10.021 --> 00:06:13.500
the low levels perception
in motion processing.

00:06:13.501 --> 00:06:14.750
Even though I did
some work on it,

00:06:14.751 --> 00:06:17.155
it just didn't feel like where
my heart was going to be.

00:06:17.156 --> 00:06:20.100
So, Dave Rumelhart, I'm
glad you mentioned him

00:06:20.101 --> 00:06:22.665
was my first real
adviser in life.

00:06:22.666 --> 00:06:25.445
He was a brilliant man,

00:06:25.446 --> 00:06:30.270
genial really, died
young unfortunately.

00:06:30.271 --> 00:06:32.130
But he set the stage

00:06:32.131 --> 00:06:33.490
for much of the work
that's going on now,

00:06:33.491 --> 00:06:35.250
and he also, I don't think

00:06:35.251 --> 00:06:37.040
would be happy with
just a narrow perspective.

00:06:37.041 --> 00:06:38.760
He really wanted to
think broadly about

00:06:38.761 --> 00:06:41.470
all the tools and all the
ways of thinking.

00:06:41.471 --> 00:06:43.670
I really got
that perspective also.

00:06:43.671 --> 00:06:44.935
He, when I first
arrived, there was

00:06:44.936 --> 00:06:46.260
interest in
schematic networks and

00:06:46.261 --> 00:06:49.440
schematics of natural
language and perception.

00:06:49.441 --> 00:06:53.240
I continue to think those are
some of the real problems,

00:06:53.241 --> 00:06:54.540
perception and abstraction,

00:06:54.541 --> 00:06:56.115
and then how do you
think about semantics.

00:06:56.116 --> 00:06:57.360
I don't think we're
there yet. I don't

00:06:57.361 --> 00:06:58.815
think we understand them.

00:06:58.816 --> 00:07:02.805
We're very, very primitive in
our understanding of those.

00:07:02.806 --> 00:07:05.790
So, then he turned
into neural nets and

00:07:05.791 --> 00:07:09.240
back propagation is
this famous algorithm,

00:07:09.241 --> 00:07:11.430
although it's really
just the chain rule,

00:07:11.431 --> 00:07:15.120
and it's kind of control
theory side to it and all that,

00:07:15.121 --> 00:07:17.990
and it's reinvented a
thousands of times.

00:07:17.991 --> 00:07:19.390
But he really is

00:07:19.391 --> 00:07:21.090
the person in the neural net
field who kind

00:07:21.091 --> 00:07:24.410
of worked on it for,
he reinvented it.

00:07:24.411 --> 00:07:26.325
For a couple of years
that was his main focus.

00:07:26.326 --> 00:07:27.630
His office was
right next to mine,

00:07:27.631 --> 00:07:29.610
and he come in every day
telling me about

00:07:29.611 --> 00:07:31.500
the cool new aspects of what he

00:07:31.501 --> 00:07:33.370
was trying to do and he
finally got the algorithm,

00:07:33.371 --> 00:07:34.860
and he tried it out
on all the Minsky

00:07:34.861 --> 00:07:36.630
papper problems and
it really did those,

00:07:36.631 --> 00:07:38.280
and he was super
excited about it.

00:07:38.281 --> 00:07:40.290
So, his excitement,
his intellectual

00:07:40.291 --> 00:07:42.320
breadth and his blend,

00:07:42.321 --> 00:07:44.610
there was no disciplinary
boundaries for Dave.

00:07:44.611 --> 00:07:47.695
Experimental psychology,
linguistics, mathematics,

00:07:47.696 --> 00:07:49.290
statistics was all fair game

00:07:49.291 --> 00:07:51.570
for his intellectual adventures.

00:07:51.571 --> 00:07:53.475
So I've taken away some of that.

00:07:53.476 --> 00:07:56.550
&gt;&gt; You talked about
descending into fields,

00:07:56.551 --> 00:07:59.940
and sucking up information
from them and growing,

00:07:59.941 --> 00:08:02.480
but you also influence
of all those fields.

00:08:02.481 --> 00:08:05.600
When I looked back
at your early work,

00:08:05.601 --> 00:08:07.800
your thesis was on
learning representations

00:08:07.801 --> 00:08:10.200
for sequential
performance essentially

00:08:10.201 --> 00:08:12.315
using recurrent neural networks

00:08:12.316 --> 00:08:16.015
for some simple cognitive tasks.

00:08:16.016 --> 00:08:17.700
One of the things
that I remember,

00:08:17.701 --> 00:08:19.805
it may have been one of
your first NIPS papers

00:08:19.806 --> 00:08:22.355
was work on adaptive
mixtures of experts,

00:08:22.356 --> 00:08:24.225
and that's something that

00:08:24.226 --> 00:08:26.400
we'll come back to at the end
because I know you have

00:08:26.401 --> 00:08:29.700
lots of important
perspectives on some of

00:08:29.701 --> 00:08:33.100
the limitations of
current deep models.

00:08:33.101 --> 00:08:38.325
But those cover are couple
of really important topics.

00:08:38.326 --> 00:08:40.304
One is looking not
just at snapshots

00:08:40.305 --> 00:08:43.170
of the world but it's
sequences of behavior,

00:08:43.171 --> 00:08:44.250
and the other is thinking not

00:08:44.251 --> 00:08:46.215
about isolated competencies but

00:08:46.216 --> 00:08:49.610
multiple experts coming together.

00:08:49.611 --> 00:08:52.530
It's amazing as I
look back at this

00:08:52.531 --> 00:08:56.435
earlier today that that
work is 30 years old now,

00:08:56.436 --> 00:08:59.040
but it really
foreshadowed some of

00:08:59.041 --> 00:09:01.800
the things that
were seeing today.

00:09:01.801 --> 00:09:04.480
&gt;&gt; Yes. Thank you for
these great questions.

00:09:04.481 --> 00:09:05.880
I'm not used to getting
such great questions.

00:09:05.881 --> 00:09:09.045
So, it was an interesting
sequential performance

00:09:09.046 --> 00:09:10.515
because the neural
nets of the day

00:09:10.516 --> 00:09:12.535
didn't do much sequentiality.

00:09:12.536 --> 00:09:17.385
It was all about matrices
and factorization and so on.

00:09:17.386 --> 00:09:19.590
But, I was pretty interesting
in the context of

00:09:19.591 --> 00:09:22.500
the psychological phenomena
which was speech.

00:09:22.580 --> 00:09:25.800
That's one of the most
important sequential acts

00:09:25.801 --> 00:09:26.880
we do is to speak.

00:09:26.881 --> 00:09:28.820
But, if you look at in detail,

00:09:28.821 --> 00:09:31.280
all of our articulator
is the tongue

00:09:31.281 --> 00:09:33.650
and the lips and
the vocal chords.

00:09:33.651 --> 00:09:36.595
All are actually staggered
and they are asynchronous.

00:09:36.596 --> 00:09:38.160
They're doing
all things in parallel.

00:09:38.161 --> 00:09:39.800
So, the feature vector of

00:09:39.801 --> 00:09:42.410
the speech apparatus has

00:09:42.411 --> 00:09:44.405
all the components
working in parallel.

00:09:44.406 --> 00:09:47.415
Nonetheless, there's an overall
sequence reality to that.

00:09:47.416 --> 00:09:49.230
So, it was very much to me,

00:09:49.231 --> 00:09:50.850
how do you model that? How
do you conceive of that?

00:09:50.851 --> 00:09:52.510
It felt like things
like neural nets were

00:09:52.511 --> 00:09:54.980
relevant because they
were naturally parallel.

00:09:54.981 --> 00:09:55.640
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:09:55.641 --> 00:09:57.330
&gt;&gt; If we can imbue them
with a bit of sequential,

00:09:57.331 --> 00:09:58.700
we can get the best
of both worlds.

00:09:58.701 --> 00:09:59.970
We could see
a little bit of that.

00:09:59.971 --> 00:10:02.300
So, actually that was
what I was interested in.

00:10:02.301 --> 00:10:03.990
I didn't really innovate,

00:10:03.991 --> 00:10:05.560
personally I don't
believe very much in

00:10:05.561 --> 00:10:07.980
the neural network sphere
in that particular line of

00:10:07.981 --> 00:10:10.830
work just using neural nets but

00:10:10.831 --> 00:10:12.560
noticing that if you train

00:10:12.561 --> 00:10:14.470
them at a particular
very sequential way,

00:10:14.471 --> 00:10:18.150
they wanted to fill
in and therefore do

00:10:18.151 --> 00:10:19.390
the core articulation or

00:10:19.391 --> 00:10:22.305
the parallelism that you
would see in real data.

00:10:22.306 --> 00:10:24.220
Again, that's kind of thing
Dave really liked.

00:10:24.221 --> 00:10:25.965
He liked that line of work that

00:10:25.966 --> 00:10:28.445
the neural net did

00:10:28.446 --> 00:10:30.715
something that was not
part of the training data,

00:10:30.716 --> 00:10:32.960
but it was reasonably
good description

00:10:32.961 --> 00:10:34.760
of something that
humans were doing.

00:10:34.761 --> 00:10:36.730
The other line of
work in fact has gone

00:10:36.731 --> 00:10:38.460
further for me which
is just thinking

00:10:38.461 --> 00:10:39.660
about modularity and abstraction

00:10:39.661 --> 00:10:40.775
but also thinking
about when you have

00:10:40.776 --> 00:10:42.955
multiple entities that have to

00:10:42.956 --> 00:10:45.965
cooperate or compete
and it's leading.

00:10:45.966 --> 00:10:48.670
So, my recent work to
much more economics perspectives

00:10:48.671 --> 00:10:50.710
and thinking about huge numbers

00:10:50.711 --> 00:10:52.830
of entities that have
to work together not

00:10:52.831 --> 00:10:56.035
just a small handful like
and are mixtures work.

00:10:56.036 --> 00:10:58.050
&gt;&gt; Okay, great. I
want to pop from

00:10:58.051 --> 00:11:01.045
your early days and it's
interesting because you've come

00:11:01.046 --> 00:11:03.635
back to some of these ideas with

00:11:03.636 --> 00:11:07.940
several decades of
advances in learning,

00:11:07.941 --> 00:11:10.135
in data, and in infrastructure.

00:11:10.136 --> 00:11:11.725
But before I do that,
I want to just talk

00:11:11.726 --> 00:11:13.330
a little bit about some of

00:11:13.331 --> 00:11:15.240
the fundamental algorithmic and

00:11:15.241 --> 00:11:17.195
theoretical advances you've made.

00:11:17.196 --> 00:11:19.480
Your work on
variational methods for

00:11:19.481 --> 00:11:21.750
approximate inference
in graphical models is

00:11:21.751 --> 00:11:24.055
huge or LDA paper.

00:11:24.056 --> 00:11:26.960
I think it's been cited
over 25,000 times.

00:11:26.961 --> 00:11:29.155
The work on spectral clustering

00:11:29.156 --> 00:11:32.305
on popularizing EM algorithms.

00:11:32.306 --> 00:11:35.540
This has really changed
the practice of

00:11:35.541 --> 00:11:41.080
machine learning and
statistics and in many ways.

00:11:41.150 --> 00:11:45.160
Do you have a gem that has not,

00:11:45.161 --> 00:11:47.560
in one of your
favorite innovations

00:11:47.561 --> 00:11:49.010
and breakthroughs
says it is not as

00:11:49.011 --> 00:11:51.020
well-recognized as some other

00:11:51.021 --> 00:11:53.900
very popular and
widely cited ones?

00:11:53.901 --> 00:11:56.525
&gt;&gt; I'm always the most fun
of my most recent work.

00:11:56.526 --> 00:11:57.015
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:11:57.016 --> 00:11:59.120
&gt;&gt; Because the older work,
it just seems obvious in

00:11:59.121 --> 00:12:02.600
this respect and probably
even obvious even in a day.

00:12:02.930 --> 00:12:07.540
But, now recent work I think
has gone the furthest.

00:12:07.541 --> 00:12:08.780
It's has become more
theoretical over

00:12:08.781 --> 00:12:10.520
time as the field has
become more empirical,

00:12:10.521 --> 00:12:11.640
things need to be

00:12:11.641 --> 00:12:14.115
a complimentary more
theoretical perspective.

00:12:14.116 --> 00:12:17.700
So, lots of work on
optimization and

00:12:17.701 --> 00:12:20.940
sampling relationships and
returning to variation themes.

00:12:20.941 --> 00:12:28.215
So, variational I learned
about it from physics,

00:12:28.216 --> 00:12:31.090
but I love the history

00:12:31.091 --> 00:12:33.550
of mathematical ideas and
variations, of course,

00:12:33.551 --> 00:12:35.210
it goes back to
variations in calculus

00:12:35.211 --> 00:12:37.260
and before and it says you turn

00:12:37.261 --> 00:12:41.220
a problem into
an optimization problem.

00:12:41.221 --> 00:12:43.680
So, you may be adjust
your problems to integrate or

00:12:43.681 --> 00:12:44.830
your problem is to write

00:12:44.831 --> 00:12:45.980
down a different
equation and you say,

00:12:45.981 --> 00:12:47.220
"Well, how can I
formulate that as

00:12:47.221 --> 00:12:49.060
a solution to an
optimization problem?"

00:12:49.061 --> 00:12:50.340
The optimization
problem could be in

00:12:50.341 --> 00:12:51.770
a space of say
probability distribution.

00:12:51.771 --> 00:12:53.930
So, your output can
be a distribution.

00:12:53.931 --> 00:12:55.440
But, now you say, "Well,
that's a solution

00:12:55.441 --> 00:12:56.740
to an optimization problem."

00:12:56.741 --> 00:12:58.340
Having done so, you get brand

00:12:58.341 --> 00:13:00.540
new mathematical perspective
and particularly you can

00:13:00.541 --> 00:13:02.395
perturb the optimization problem

00:13:02.396 --> 00:13:04.610
that will then move
the solution a little bit,

00:13:04.611 --> 00:13:09.255
and the perturbation can
lead you to insight.

00:13:09.256 --> 00:13:11.900
But, it also can lead
you to new algorithms.

00:13:11.901 --> 00:13:14.670
So, there is a variational
perspectives to

00:13:14.671 --> 00:13:16.945
all sort of things,
calculating an eigenvector.

00:13:16.946 --> 00:13:20.425
An eigenvector as ax equals
lambda x but it's also

00:13:20.426 --> 00:13:22.730
a relay ratio where you optimize

00:13:22.731 --> 00:13:24.750
a certain ratio and
that is an eigenvector.

00:13:24.751 --> 00:13:27.180
It's a variational characterization
eigenvector formula.

00:13:27.181 --> 00:13:28.955
In that way, you
get new algorithms.

00:13:28.956 --> 00:13:32.500
So, the physicists
were onto this.

00:13:32.501 --> 00:13:34.870
So, people had
borrowed the idea of

00:13:34.871 --> 00:13:37.500
Mean-Field methods from
physics into neural nets.

00:13:37.501 --> 00:13:40.590
So, there was lots of these
Mean-Field are deterministic.

00:13:41.310 --> 00:13:44.220
I found that very
provocative even started

00:13:44.221 --> 00:13:46.600
reading deeper and as often was

00:13:46.601 --> 00:13:49.650
the case they had
20 years of history and

00:13:49.651 --> 00:13:51.050
a push that much further and they

00:13:51.051 --> 00:13:53.790
started to call that
variational methods.

00:13:53.850 --> 00:13:57.110
So, we borrowed I think
the broader perspective and

00:13:57.111 --> 00:14:00.040
then brought that into context
with graphical models.

00:14:00.041 --> 00:14:05.105
They mainly were studying
lattices and physics things.

00:14:05.106 --> 00:14:06.950
So, we said, "Well,
there's an opportunity

00:14:06.951 --> 00:14:08.670
to take those same kind
of ideas and bring them

00:14:08.671 --> 00:14:10.500
into the much more
structured world of

00:14:10.501 --> 00:14:13.385
graphical models and
particular directed graphs."

00:14:13.386 --> 00:14:16.005
So, conditional
probabilities rather

00:14:16.006 --> 00:14:17.870
than just field models.

00:14:17.871 --> 00:14:19.320
So, in some level,

00:14:19.321 --> 00:14:21.160
it was just explorations in

00:14:21.161 --> 00:14:23.385
the set of ideas that hadn't
quite brought together,

00:14:23.386 --> 00:14:27.245
then EMI day also there
hadn't been much work on

00:14:27.246 --> 00:14:33.075
doing them with the graphical
model variational things.

00:14:33.076 --> 00:14:34.960
So, but that still
goes for to this day.

00:14:34.961 --> 00:14:36.450
That line of work I'm actually

00:14:36.451 --> 00:14:38.140
still feel is very,
very incomplete.

00:14:38.141 --> 00:14:39.730
There's hardly any theory on it.

00:14:39.731 --> 00:14:41.140
There's finally starting to

00:14:41.141 --> 00:14:42.670
be a little more innovation
algorithmically.

00:14:42.671 --> 00:14:46.450
Again, it's picking up again
but these things are a

00:14:46.451 --> 00:14:48.915
really long-term projects for

00:14:48.916 --> 00:14:50.610
not just me but for
the whole community.

00:14:50.611 --> 00:14:52.260
&gt;&gt; You've been doing some work

00:14:52.261 --> 00:14:54.505
recently on non-convex
optimization?

00:14:54.506 --> 00:14:54.900
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:14:54.901 --> 00:14:56.570
&gt;&gt; Can you talk a little bit
about that because

00:14:56.571 --> 00:14:58.570
a lot of the functions
clearly that

00:14:58.571 --> 00:15:00.440
you're trying to
optimize especially in

00:15:00.441 --> 00:15:03.780
deep models are not
particularly convex.

00:15:03.781 --> 00:15:05.150
&gt;&gt; Well, not just deep models.

00:15:05.151 --> 00:15:07.390
So, all kinds of
phased retrieval models,

00:15:07.391 --> 00:15:09.070
all kinds of
combinatorial models,

00:15:09.071 --> 00:15:10.820
a lot of them are non-convex.

00:15:10.821 --> 00:15:12.950
They have a single global
optimum, some of them.

00:15:12.951 --> 00:15:13.360
&gt;&gt; Yeah, of course.

00:15:13.361 --> 00:15:14.430
&gt;&gt; So, they seem simple but they

00:15:14.431 --> 00:15:15.950
have all the saddle points.

00:15:15.951 --> 00:15:18.240
The neural nets and I think

00:15:18.241 --> 00:15:20.740
that one of the key problems

00:15:20.741 --> 00:15:22.735
is they have lots
of saddle points.

00:15:22.736 --> 00:15:24.620
I don't think this was
appreciate at least in

00:15:24.621 --> 00:15:26.260
my era back when I was
learning neural nets,

00:15:26.261 --> 00:15:28.620
but it was all about
the local optima that should

00:15:28.621 --> 00:15:29.910
fall into in the simulated

00:15:29.911 --> 00:15:31.930
annealing and ways of
getting out of that.

00:15:31.931 --> 00:15:33.930
I think it's in the last five

00:15:33.931 --> 00:15:35.350
years or so that
it's been realized,

00:15:35.351 --> 00:15:36.520
well if there are a few local men

00:15:36.521 --> 00:15:37.620
or even a whole lot
there could be

00:15:37.621 --> 00:15:40.130
even more saddle points
and what'll happen as

00:15:40.131 --> 00:15:42.390
it high dimensions get stuck
next to the saddle points

00:15:42.391 --> 00:15:45.120
for indeterminate
amount of time and it

00:15:45.121 --> 00:15:47.600
could be even scale

00:15:47.601 --> 00:15:50.490
badly in dimensions so that
if in bigger problems,

00:15:50.491 --> 00:15:52.395
you'll spend more and more time
around the saddle points

00:15:52.396 --> 00:15:54.295
and you will know that because
not just saddle point,

00:15:54.296 --> 00:15:57.755
you just feel the learning curve
is flattened out,

00:15:57.756 --> 00:15:59.880
and you think you're done,
but you really just have,

00:15:59.881 --> 00:16:02.180
you got to escape from
that direction of escape.

00:16:02.181 --> 00:16:04.050
So, let me mention Shrijan who's

00:16:04.051 --> 00:16:05.770
a student with me
working at Berkeley.

00:16:05.771 --> 00:16:07.870
He's a real leader in this area

00:16:07.871 --> 00:16:10.020
of saddle point
avoidance and escape.

00:16:10.021 --> 00:16:11.730
There is a sequence
of papers where he is

00:16:11.731 --> 00:16:14.330
the lead author
that you really dig

00:16:14.331 --> 00:16:16.380
into this and try to
get not just rates of

00:16:16.381 --> 00:16:18.905
convergence but
dimension dependence.

00:16:18.906 --> 00:16:21.770
So, it's kind of pride
in theoretical work.

00:16:21.771 --> 00:16:24.090
I think this is not just me
but the rest of our field has

00:16:24.091 --> 00:16:26.690
gotten very good at
not just asymptotic rates,

00:16:26.691 --> 00:16:27.880
but actually finite sample rates.

00:16:27.881 --> 00:16:29.990
Then beyond that, the constant

00:16:29.991 --> 00:16:32.170
has dimension dependence and
we're getting the constants.

00:16:32.171 --> 00:16:34.130
We're getting sort of
the dimension dependence.

00:16:34.131 --> 00:16:34.700
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:16:34.701 --> 00:16:35.910
&gt;&gt; So, that's a theory that

00:16:35.911 --> 00:16:37.680
becomes extremely
interesting mathematically

00:16:37.681 --> 00:16:40.240
but also very real in
practice and gives this

00:16:40.241 --> 00:16:41.630
field a lot of

00:16:41.631 --> 00:16:44.140
connections that didn't
exist five or 10 years ago.

00:16:44.141 --> 00:16:45.555
So, saddle points are interesting

00:16:45.556 --> 00:16:47.355
if you want to avoid them.

00:16:47.356 --> 00:16:48.490
Indeed, there's lots of

00:16:48.491 --> 00:16:50.070
non-convex optimization
problems too.

00:16:50.071 --> 00:16:51.800
But, it is also
interesting if you take

00:16:51.801 --> 00:16:54.060
the game from this perspective
or multiple agents.

00:16:54.061 --> 00:16:55.550
Where you don't want to
avoid saddle points,

00:16:55.551 --> 00:16:57.200
you want to find
the saddle points,

00:16:57.201 --> 00:16:59.110
because the saddle points
through the Nash equilibria

00:16:59.111 --> 00:17:00.795
or the other kind of equilibria.

00:17:00.796 --> 00:17:03.000
So, that's an emerging area

00:17:03.001 --> 00:17:05.385
that I'm working on with
students in my group.

00:17:05.386 --> 00:17:08.860
There is a literature
about how to go

00:17:08.861 --> 00:17:10.125
towards saddle points but

00:17:10.126 --> 00:17:12.160
as is often the case, it's
incomplete literature.

00:17:12.161 --> 00:17:15.000
It didn't face the modern
constraints that we have.

00:17:15.001 --> 00:17:16.650
In particular, if you create

00:17:16.651 --> 00:17:18.405
saddle points that
are Nash equilibria,

00:17:18.406 --> 00:17:19.495
you'll probably create lots of

00:17:19.496 --> 00:17:20.960
other saddle points
that you don't want to

00:17:20.961 --> 00:17:24.105
hit just because that's how
mathematics tends to work.

00:17:24.106 --> 00:17:25.945
So, there's great open

00:17:25.946 --> 00:17:27.830
problems there and
then if you go to

00:17:27.831 --> 00:17:30.030
the large scale versions of
these things where you have

00:17:30.031 --> 00:17:31.420
not just two agents or 10

00:17:31.421 --> 00:17:33.280
but you've got
hundreds of thousands,

00:17:33.281 --> 00:17:35.810
it's going to be very, very
interesting and challenging.

00:17:35.811 --> 00:17:37.830
So, that's one aspect of

00:17:37.831 --> 00:17:39.180
the economics perspective meets

00:17:39.181 --> 00:17:40.820
algorithms that I'm
very interested in.

00:17:40.821 --> 00:17:42.640
&gt;&gt; Okay. Great, thank you.

00:17:42.641 --> 00:17:46.690
Let's try to talk about
a couple of other topics.

00:17:46.691 --> 00:17:49.230
So, one is in
the last decade or so,

00:17:49.231 --> 00:17:52.160
we've seen just
dramatic advances in

00:17:52.161 --> 00:17:55.925
the capabilities of
computational systems

00:17:55.926 --> 00:17:58.115
to do things like
understand speech,

00:17:58.116 --> 00:18:01.160
to understand aspects of vision,

00:18:01.161 --> 00:18:04.300
label objects, generate captions,

00:18:04.301 --> 00:18:06.270
to do some aspects of

00:18:06.271 --> 00:18:09.035
language understanding
particularly the work

00:18:09.036 --> 00:18:11.875
in machine translation
I think has

00:18:11.876 --> 00:18:15.259
been really beyond
my expectations,

00:18:15.260 --> 00:18:17.165
some work in robotic control

00:18:17.166 --> 00:18:19.640
and many of
these innovations have

00:18:19.641 --> 00:18:22.390
fueled I think things
that neither of us would

00:18:22.391 --> 00:18:25.120
have expected 10 or 15 years ago.

00:18:25.121 --> 00:18:26.220
The fact that you can do

00:18:26.221 --> 00:18:29.850
real-time analysis of
voice on phones in

00:18:29.851 --> 00:18:32.265
noisy environments and get it

00:18:32.266 --> 00:18:34.920
right a startling
proportion of the time.

00:18:34.921 --> 00:18:37.480
The fact that in
machine translation,

00:18:37.481 --> 00:18:39.190
you can translate
from one language to

00:18:39.191 --> 00:18:41.395
another in near real-time.

00:18:41.396 --> 00:18:44.150
These accomplishments
have fueled a variety of

00:18:44.151 --> 00:18:47.080
interesting new
computational capabilities

00:18:47.081 --> 00:18:55.660
yet they have a set of
very isolated competencies.

00:18:55.661 --> 00:19:00.075
You recently published
an op-ed piece or

00:19:00.076 --> 00:19:04.380
thought piece in a medium
on Artificial Intelligence,

00:19:04.381 --> 00:19:05.950
the revolution that
hasn't happened

00:19:05.951 --> 00:19:08.580
yet that generated a lot of buzz.

00:19:08.581 --> 00:19:12.040
Can you reflect on the fact
that there have been

00:19:12.041 --> 00:19:17.310
amazing advances yet we're miles

00:19:17.311 --> 00:19:19.360
and miles and miles away

00:19:19.361 --> 00:19:24.060
from thinking about intelligence
in the same way that

00:19:24.061 --> 00:19:26.035
you see in humans or

00:19:26.036 --> 00:19:28.690
more interestingly
interacting sets

00:19:28.691 --> 00:19:31.510
of humans and
computational elements.

00:19:31.511 --> 00:19:33.265
&gt;&gt; Great question again.

00:19:33.266 --> 00:19:34.830
So, there's a lot of issues there

00:19:34.831 --> 00:19:36.535
that need to be untangled and so

00:19:36.536 --> 00:19:38.515
I'll try not to speak too long

00:19:38.516 --> 00:19:40.500
but in my op-ed tries
to untangle.

00:19:40.501 --> 00:19:42.085
I think that everyone is

00:19:42.086 --> 00:19:44.510
lumped too much
into the phrase AI.

00:19:44.511 --> 00:19:46.560
One of my the phrases I

00:19:46.561 --> 00:19:48.110
was proud about in
the op-ed was that

00:19:48.111 --> 00:19:51.985
people are using AI as
an intellectual wildcard.

00:19:51.986 --> 00:19:53.990
That when they don't
understand what's

00:19:53.991 --> 00:19:55.860
happening or understand
how to solve it,

00:19:55.861 --> 00:19:57.810
they say, "AI will do

00:19:57.811 --> 00:20:00.265
it or that's AI problem
or whatever."

00:20:00.266 --> 00:20:03.450
Most of these systems are
pattern recognition systems

00:20:03.451 --> 00:20:04.650
and that's great.

00:20:04.651 --> 00:20:05.650
There are lots of patterns in

00:20:05.651 --> 00:20:07.210
the world and these things
are pretty good

00:20:07.211 --> 00:20:08.310
if you have especially lots of

00:20:08.311 --> 00:20:10.270
labels that help you
find those patterns.

00:20:10.271 --> 00:20:11.900
I, first of all,

00:20:11.901 --> 00:20:13.830
don't think that's
the whole class problems

00:20:13.831 --> 00:20:15.645
I'm interested in or many of
us should be interested in.

00:20:15.646 --> 00:20:18.090
I think that creating

00:20:18.091 --> 00:20:20.410
systems that serve
humans and help us make

00:20:20.411 --> 00:20:23.540
our lives safer more
interesting do this

00:20:23.541 --> 00:20:24.850
at scale make us able to

00:20:24.851 --> 00:20:26.985
interact with other humans
in the world better.

00:20:26.986 --> 00:20:29.860
Very few of those have
a lot to do with creating

00:20:29.861 --> 00:20:34.120
artificial humans with speech
language vision problems.

00:20:34.121 --> 00:20:36.220
They have to do with all kinds

00:20:36.221 --> 00:20:37.700
of decision-making at scale,

00:20:37.701 --> 00:20:38.935
they have to do with

00:20:38.936 --> 00:20:41.315
problems and none of
us evolve to solve.

00:20:41.316 --> 00:20:43.150
That our human intelligence isn't

00:20:43.151 --> 00:20:44.615
particularly good at and

00:20:44.616 --> 00:20:47.380
whereas good decision-making
inferential principles

00:20:47.381 --> 00:20:49.055
are going to be needed
to build such systems.

00:20:49.056 --> 00:20:51.580
If you think of domains
like commerce or healthcare

00:20:51.581 --> 00:20:54.610
or finance or et cetera,

00:20:54.611 --> 00:20:56.880
there's just crying
out for here's data,

00:20:56.881 --> 00:21:00.090
here's utilities with
large numbers of players.

00:21:00.091 --> 00:21:02.610
How do I create something
that will work for people?

00:21:02.611 --> 00:21:05.560
So, I don't think that working on

00:21:05.561 --> 00:21:08.160
what I called in that article
"human-imitative AI".

00:21:08.161 --> 00:21:13.130
The classical aspiration
which is still an aspiration.

00:21:13.131 --> 00:21:15.470
I don't think it's necessary
for sufficient for

00:21:15.471 --> 00:21:18.000
working on a lot of
these challenges.

00:21:18.001 --> 00:21:19.785
So, lumping them all together,

00:21:19.786 --> 00:21:22.530
I think is a mistake.

00:21:24.800 --> 00:21:27.510
The biggest progress
has been made in areas

00:21:27.511 --> 00:21:28.860
like you're talking
about vision and

00:21:28.861 --> 00:21:31.280
speech and natural
language and so on.

00:21:31.281 --> 00:21:33.200
But the algorithms that are used

00:21:33.201 --> 00:21:34.590
for that are not necessarily
the ones that are

00:21:34.591 --> 00:21:35.605
going to work for trying to make

00:21:35.606 --> 00:21:37.470
100,000 decisions at scale.

00:21:37.471 --> 00:21:43.670
So, it is I

00:21:43.671 --> 00:21:44.975
think unfortunate to
think that those are

00:21:44.976 --> 00:21:48.205
the main problems that industry
should be focusing on.

00:21:48.206 --> 00:21:49.480
There's a larger class of

00:21:49.481 --> 00:21:51.080
problems they should
be focusing on.

00:21:51.081 --> 00:21:53.310
Now, also I do need to push back.

00:21:53.311 --> 00:21:55.630
You said the word speech
understanding and

00:21:55.631 --> 00:21:57.640
language understanding
and intelligence

00:21:57.641 --> 00:21:59.010
may even have come up there.

00:21:59.011 --> 00:22:00.680
I don't think these systems and

00:22:00.681 --> 00:22:02.180
with all due respect for
my colleagues who are

00:22:02.181 --> 00:22:03.070
in those areas are doing

00:22:03.071 --> 00:22:05.190
much speech understanding, right?

00:22:05.191 --> 00:22:07.020
They're transcribing a way form

00:22:07.021 --> 00:22:09.360
into lexical items, right?

00:22:09.361 --> 00:22:11.730
Very impressively with
lots of noise and all

00:22:11.731 --> 00:22:15.005
that but they don't understand
anything about the speech.

00:22:15.006 --> 00:22:18.525
Moreover, the natural
language translation systems

00:22:18.526 --> 00:22:20.710
are not understanding
the language at all.

00:22:20.711 --> 00:22:21.755
They are taking in

00:22:21.756 --> 00:22:23.755
a French sentence and
basically they are

00:22:23.756 --> 00:22:27.350
mapping it that string
to an English string.

00:22:27.351 --> 00:22:29.680
All right. There's
no understanding

00:22:29.681 --> 00:22:31.155
between here and there.

00:22:31.156 --> 00:22:33.035
Now, when we look
at the outputs of

00:22:33.036 --> 00:22:34.265
these things and the errors

00:22:34.266 --> 00:22:35.495
and the kind of
things they could do,

00:22:35.496 --> 00:22:36.820
we as humans are able to

00:22:36.821 --> 00:22:38.300
sort of read into
them saying, "Wow!

00:22:38.301 --> 00:22:40.650
It seemed to understand
something." Well, no.

00:22:40.651 --> 00:22:42.530
There was a statistical
thing that it picked

00:22:42.531 --> 00:22:44.480
up on and that's not
all it is that's not.

00:22:44.481 --> 00:22:46.380
So, our ability to understand
what's happening in

00:22:46.381 --> 00:22:48.680
the world to abstract
in particular,

00:22:48.681 --> 00:22:51.015
we are miraculous abstractors

00:22:51.016 --> 00:22:53.200
and neural nets do a little bit

00:22:53.201 --> 00:22:54.585
of abstraction in their feature

00:22:54.586 --> 00:22:56.870
finding but they
don't think about

00:22:56.871 --> 00:23:00.145
the high-level

00:23:00.146 --> 00:23:05.065
multifaceted complex
abstractions humans are.

00:23:05.066 --> 00:23:07.325
&gt;&gt; I mean to be fair, a lot
of human capabilities

00:23:07.326 --> 00:23:10.390
especially perceptual
capabilities are

00:23:10.391 --> 00:23:12.840
based on statistical
regularities of the world.

00:23:12.841 --> 00:23:13.940
&gt;&gt; Well, they could be based on

00:23:13.941 --> 00:23:15.060
that we don't know if they are.

00:23:15.061 --> 00:23:17.760
I mean so think about a squirrel.

00:23:17.761 --> 00:23:19.350
I talked about this
in the op-ed too.

00:23:19.351 --> 00:23:24.230
But a squirrel is an amazing
perceptual motor system.

00:23:24.231 --> 00:23:26.305
It hops among
the trees in ways that

00:23:26.306 --> 00:23:28.190
we can even have
hardly imagine, right?

00:23:28.191 --> 00:23:31.030
So, it's doing somehow
it's brain has

00:23:31.031 --> 00:23:34.085
evolved or maybe learned but
probably not learned a lot.

00:23:34.086 --> 00:23:35.740
Probably, a lot of evolution

00:23:35.741 --> 00:23:37.600
came into making that
squirrel so good.

00:23:37.601 --> 00:23:40.110
So, it's able to do things
that we can hardly fathom.

00:23:40.111 --> 00:23:42.480
There are neural nets or
maybe starting to approach

00:23:42.481 --> 00:23:43.625
the end of our lifetimes we'll

00:23:43.626 --> 00:23:45.555
be able to see a lot
from squirrels.

00:23:45.556 --> 00:23:47.710
I would hesitate though
to say squirrel has

00:23:47.711 --> 00:23:49.920
a lot of intelligence, all right.

00:23:49.921 --> 00:23:51.920
Building such as
a squirrel-like system would

00:23:51.921 --> 00:23:52.920
require a vast amount of

00:23:52.921 --> 00:23:54.715
intelligence or
human intelligence.

00:23:54.716 --> 00:23:56.740
But I hesitate to say
that that system is going

00:23:56.741 --> 00:23:58.620
to be one that
embodies what I would

00:23:58.621 --> 00:24:01.565
recognize as intelligence
or understanding of

00:24:01.566 --> 00:24:02.995
the world or be able to abstract

00:24:02.996 --> 00:24:04.585
about it. I think squirrels.

00:24:04.586 --> 00:24:06.160
We don't want to limit
what squirrels can do.

00:24:06.161 --> 00:24:07.850
They can probably do some

00:24:07.851 --> 00:24:09.460
problem-solving and annoying us

00:24:09.461 --> 00:24:11.770
with their abilities
in that regard.

00:24:11.771 --> 00:24:14.950
But really so,

00:24:14.951 --> 00:24:17.105
if you haven't seen
Doug Hofstadter's thing on,

00:24:17.106 --> 00:24:19.190
he had an op-ed as well
around the same time I wrote

00:24:19.191 --> 00:24:21.475
mine on The Shallowness
of Google Translate,

00:24:21.476 --> 00:24:24.130
and I think he does
a pretty good job of saying,

00:24:24.131 --> 00:24:25.810
"If you don't have
any semantics at all,

00:24:25.811 --> 00:24:27.035
you're just not going
to really be able to

00:24:27.036 --> 00:24:28.680
solve natural language
translation."

00:24:28.681 --> 00:24:31.320
So, you get a sense that
people think well we got went

00:24:31.321 --> 00:24:34.340
from 80 percent to 90 to 95.

00:24:34.341 --> 00:24:36.765
Of course, we're going
to be to 99.9 soon

00:24:36.766 --> 00:24:39.630
just more hardware or
more data will do it.

00:24:39.631 --> 00:24:42.340
That's just is not true in
a domain where it really

00:24:42.341 --> 00:24:43.640
requires a rich understanding of

00:24:43.641 --> 00:24:45.480
the world around us to
really do what's right.

00:24:45.481 --> 00:24:49.000
&gt;&gt; I think it's really
clear that there are

00:24:49.001 --> 00:24:52.030
huge limitations to
the competencies

00:24:52.031 --> 00:24:54.870
that current systems have.

00:24:55.890 --> 00:24:58.390
To get to the next level,

00:24:58.391 --> 00:25:01.295
we need to think more
broadly about systems that

00:25:01.296 --> 00:25:08.160
have perceptual or low-level
recognition capabilities

00:25:08.161 --> 00:25:10.900
as well as more symbolic
deductive reasoning.

00:25:10.901 --> 00:25:12.930
&gt;&gt; Well, they may be symbolic
deductive, they may not.

00:25:12.931 --> 00:25:15.100
Abstraction is a word
that doesn't have to

00:25:15.101 --> 00:25:19.140
be- the idea that we're
having an interview,

00:25:19.141 --> 00:25:20.900
that's an abstraction of
what is happening with

00:25:20.901 --> 00:25:22.020
all the pixels and all the sound

00:25:22.021 --> 00:25:23.195
waves in the room right now.

00:25:23.196 --> 00:25:24.700
It's not a low-level abstraction,

00:25:24.701 --> 00:25:25.490
it's quite a high-level.

00:25:25.491 --> 00:25:27.230
There's many, many kinds
of interviews and many,

00:25:27.231 --> 00:25:28.620
many kinds of
situations that all of

00:25:28.621 --> 00:25:30.735
us humans really
understand that thing.

00:25:30.736 --> 00:25:34.400
I also noticed on the last time
I came here to Microsoft,

00:25:34.401 --> 00:25:35.620
I was participating
in an event called

00:25:35.621 --> 00:25:37.355
the Disruptive Technology Review.

00:25:37.356 --> 00:25:38.955
&gt;&gt; That's right.

00:25:38.956 --> 00:25:41.620
&gt;&gt; I know what disruption
means but it now has

00:25:41.621 --> 00:25:42.710
all kinds of other meanings

00:25:42.711 --> 00:25:43.940
that people have
associated with it.

00:25:43.941 --> 00:25:46.205
It's become a good thing,
first of all,

00:25:46.206 --> 00:25:48.380
and all of us understand that and

00:25:48.381 --> 00:25:50.890
disruptive technology really
can be parsed in two ways.

00:25:50.891 --> 00:25:53.585
It could be a disruptive
technology review.

00:25:53.586 --> 00:25:56.055
We're reviewing
the disruptive technology

00:25:56.056 --> 00:25:58.890
or it could be a disruptive
technology review.

00:25:58.891 --> 00:26:00.810
All right. We'll get that and

00:26:00.811 --> 00:26:02.710
it's interesting even
funny and amusing.

00:26:02.711 --> 00:26:04.235
You probably didn't
think about it.

00:26:04.236 --> 00:26:05.190
You probably picked one of those

00:26:05.191 --> 00:26:06.590
two but they're both there.

00:26:06.591 --> 00:26:08.720
Our computers are very,

00:26:08.721 --> 00:26:11.440
very far from the ability
to understand things of

00:26:11.441 --> 00:26:14.005
that sense and have humor

00:26:14.006 --> 00:26:16.795
about them and use
them in creative ways.

00:26:16.796 --> 00:26:18.870
Do that not just for
the words disruptive technology

00:26:18.871 --> 00:26:21.195
but for about every other word
that we can think of.

00:26:21.196 --> 00:26:23.310
Even the word saying
that bad is now

00:26:23.311 --> 00:26:25.325
good and stuff like
that as humans really,

00:26:25.326 --> 00:26:26.920
really are flexible with

00:26:26.921 --> 00:26:28.470
the ability to use that and
that's not even talking

00:26:28.471 --> 00:26:29.490
about how we interact with

00:26:29.491 --> 00:26:30.805
the real world and

00:26:30.806 --> 00:26:32.720
the abstractions that
are involved there.

00:26:32.721 --> 00:26:36.460
So, these aren't problems
that one can't work on,

00:26:36.461 --> 00:26:39.640
but it's just decades
and it's not that

00:26:39.641 --> 00:26:40.880
we get enough big datasets and

00:26:40.881 --> 00:26:42.440
enough hardware and we get it.

00:26:42.441 --> 00:26:43.970
I just think there's
a little there's

00:26:43.971 --> 00:26:45.820
a lot of naivete to
tell you about that.

00:26:45.821 --> 00:26:47.805
My colleagues who work
on those problems in

00:26:47.806 --> 00:26:48.940
natural language don't have that

00:26:48.941 --> 00:26:50.780
naivete for the most part but

00:26:50.781 --> 00:26:52.890
a lot of the PR agents

00:26:52.891 --> 00:26:54.190
for sort of the companies
that are doing

00:26:54.191 --> 00:26:55.875
this do have
that naivete and that's

00:26:55.876 --> 00:26:58.445
being spread out too
far into the community.

00:26:58.446 --> 00:27:02.110
&gt;&gt; Do you have any reflections
on what's needed to get

00:27:02.111 --> 00:27:05.900
to some of the competencies that?

00:27:05.901 --> 00:27:07.315
&gt;&gt; Well, I can give my own.

00:27:07.316 --> 00:27:08.670
I mean there's just a

00:27:08.671 --> 00:27:10.290
huge number in fact
in some of my talks,

00:27:10.291 --> 00:27:13.160
they give a long list
of challenges for AI.

00:27:13.161 --> 00:27:14.300
It's not just that
you need symbols,

00:27:14.301 --> 00:27:15.815
you need all sorts
of other things.

00:27:15.816 --> 00:27:18.015
But I mean I think I'm going to

00:27:18.016 --> 00:27:20.775
try to avoid your question
just because I

00:27:20.776 --> 00:27:25.280
dabble in NLP but
certainly relations

00:27:25.281 --> 00:27:32.060
and certainly a minimal
amount of first order things.

00:27:32.061 --> 00:27:34.470
Certainly, the ability
to deal with hierarchies

00:27:34.471 --> 00:27:36.130
and the ability to deal

00:27:36.131 --> 00:27:38.435
with abstractions
at various levels.

00:27:38.436 --> 00:27:42.300
I think we don't really know
what we really need yet.

00:27:42.301 --> 00:27:45.095
So, I think it's like
someone who was able to,

00:27:45.096 --> 00:27:47.730
the first people who had
gunpowder and could build

00:27:47.731 --> 00:27:49.925
a very minimal rocket and

00:27:49.926 --> 00:27:52.715
then could dream about going
to the moon someday, right?

00:27:52.716 --> 00:27:54.210
We did go to the moon
some day but there was

00:27:54.211 --> 00:27:55.590
a whole lot of other new concepts

00:27:55.591 --> 00:27:56.920
that had to be
invented to actually

00:27:56.921 --> 00:27:59.120
get there all
the way to the moon.

00:27:59.121 --> 00:28:01.910
So, I think we're
leaving lots of problems

00:28:01.911 --> 00:28:04.100
for the younger generation.

00:28:04.101 --> 00:28:06.180
&gt;&gt; That's always good.

00:28:06.181 --> 00:28:08.170
&gt;&gt; Even if a neural
net could take,

00:28:08.171 --> 00:28:11.170
it can do Arabic to English
translation at 99 percent,

00:28:11.171 --> 00:28:13.275
which I don't believe
that's going to happen,

00:28:13.276 --> 00:28:14.600
it's going to really,
at that point,

00:28:14.601 --> 00:28:15.880
be such a huge black box.

00:28:15.881 --> 00:28:17.690
This is not healthy for

00:28:17.691 --> 00:28:18.460
us to turn that over to

00:28:18.461 --> 00:28:19.630
the next generation and
say, "Here, we're done.

00:28:19.631 --> 00:28:21.235
We did natural
language translation."

00:28:21.236 --> 00:28:22.340
They're not going
to be very happy

00:28:22.341 --> 00:28:24.140
about that and they shouldn't be.

00:28:24.141 --> 00:28:26.360
Let me just add one more point
because I don't want to

00:28:26.361 --> 00:28:28.505
come across as
negative about this.

00:28:28.506 --> 00:28:30.105
This is a great fact

00:28:30.106 --> 00:28:32.680
that neural nets are

00:28:32.681 --> 00:28:35.065
an amazingly good pattern
recognition class versus.

00:28:35.066 --> 00:28:36.320
That also means you can use

00:28:36.321 --> 00:28:37.715
these for real world problems.

00:28:37.716 --> 00:28:38.040
&gt;&gt; Correct.

00:28:38.041 --> 00:28:41.340
&gt;&gt; So, the fact
that we have okay,

00:28:41.341 --> 00:28:44.055
but not particularly
good translation

00:28:44.056 --> 00:28:47.350
means that I could probably
now take a device,

00:28:47.351 --> 00:28:49.880
I could speak into it and
out would come bad Mandarin.

00:28:49.881 --> 00:28:51.920
Therefore, I can walk around
in China and I can name it

00:28:51.921 --> 00:28:52.970
actually like I could
have never had;

00:28:52.971 --> 00:28:53.910
and that's good for me,

00:28:53.911 --> 00:28:56.290
it's good for society, it's
good for humans that things

00:28:56.291 --> 00:28:58.940
like that can be done. All right.

00:28:58.941 --> 00:29:00.315
But it tends to lead into

00:29:00.316 --> 00:29:01.950
people's minds way
further than that.

00:29:01.951 --> 00:29:03.040
They think, "Well,
we've almost got

00:29:03.041 --> 00:29:04.390
natural language
translation done,

00:29:04.391 --> 00:29:06.355
bla bla bla bla, where
is that lead to?"

00:29:06.356 --> 00:29:08.785
And the fact is "Well, we're
not even close to really.

00:29:08.786 --> 00:29:11.365
We're like one percent of
really being able to do like

00:29:11.366 --> 00:29:12.960
a human being can

00:29:12.961 --> 00:29:14.390
do like a Shakespeare
can write Shakespeare",

00:29:14.391 --> 00:29:16.360
but even like my 11 year old

00:29:16.361 --> 00:29:19.225
can interact with his world
using natural language.

00:29:19.226 --> 00:29:20.470
&gt;&gt; Eighteen-month olds
can interact

00:29:20.471 --> 00:29:21.825
with their world in ways

00:29:21.826 --> 00:29:25.155
that many of our current
systems can't do.

00:29:25.156 --> 00:29:31.965
I want to read you
a quote from the AI.

00:29:31.966 --> 00:29:33.680
&gt;&gt; I feel like I'm
suddenly in a courtroom.

00:29:33.681 --> 00:29:36.130
&gt;&gt; No. No. No. This is
a good one I think.

00:29:36.131 --> 00:29:37.485
&gt;&gt; Five years ago Mike,

00:29:37.486 --> 00:29:41.980
you said this and now
you're saying that.

00:29:41.981 --> 00:29:43.940
&gt;&gt; Just as humans
built buildings and

00:29:43.941 --> 00:29:46.165
bridges before there
was civil engineering,

00:29:46.166 --> 00:29:49.240
humans are processing
with the building of

00:29:49.241 --> 00:29:51.150
societal scale inference
and decision

00:29:51.151 --> 00:29:53.450
making systems that
involve machines,

00:29:53.451 --> 00:29:54.920
humans and the environment.

00:29:54.921 --> 00:29:58.015
Just this early building and
bridges sometimes fell to

00:29:58.016 --> 00:30:02.510
the ground in unforeseen ways
with tragic consequences,

00:30:02.511 --> 00:30:04.180
many of our early societal

00:30:04.181 --> 00:30:05.800
scale inference and
decision making

00:30:05.801 --> 00:30:09.630
systems are already exposing
serious conceptual flaws.

00:30:09.631 --> 00:30:11.130
You look into the a little bit of

00:30:11.131 --> 00:30:12.710
that in your previous comment,

00:30:12.711 --> 00:30:13.930
but this really focuses on

00:30:13.931 --> 00:30:19.150
these larger systems issues
and systems ways of thinking.

00:30:19.151 --> 00:30:22.910
Can you comment on some
of that fragility?

00:30:22.911 --> 00:30:28.830
How we're setting up people to

00:30:28.831 --> 00:30:31.040
think about what's possible

00:30:31.041 --> 00:30:32.545
and really what steps we need to

00:30:32.546 --> 00:30:36.500
take to make progress
in some of these areas.

00:30:36.501 --> 00:30:39.985
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, I attempted in

00:30:39.986 --> 00:30:41.820
this op-ed to give

00:30:41.821 --> 00:30:44.580
Injaring the due respect
that it needs.

00:30:44.581 --> 00:30:46.630
Injaring is not just a bunch of

00:30:46.631 --> 00:30:48.825
nerds playing around with wires.

00:30:48.826 --> 00:30:50.195
Injaring is really thinking

00:30:50.196 --> 00:30:51.690
about how to make
the world a better place,

00:30:51.691 --> 00:30:53.600
and how to make it a
little more predictable,

00:30:53.601 --> 00:30:55.140
a little more
interesting and so on.

00:30:55.141 --> 00:30:57.090
So, I have great respect

00:30:57.091 --> 00:30:58.555
for people who did
civil engineering,

00:30:58.556 --> 00:31:00.225
chemical engineering
in past generations.

00:31:00.226 --> 00:31:02.055
My father was
a chemical engineer,

00:31:02.056 --> 00:31:04.485
and they started with
chemical principles.

00:31:04.486 --> 00:31:05.790
You blend these test tubes

00:31:05.791 --> 00:31:06.870
together and out comes something,

00:31:06.871 --> 00:31:08.290
and they went from there
all the way to be able

00:31:08.291 --> 00:31:10.195
to put this together
in a factory.

00:31:10.196 --> 00:31:13.030
They needed a huge raft
of principles to do that,

00:31:13.031 --> 00:31:14.470
not just the basic chemistry

00:31:14.471 --> 00:31:15.880
but all kinds of
mechanical things

00:31:15.881 --> 00:31:19.010
and a range of other things
I know very little about.

00:31:19.011 --> 00:31:21.610
That took decades to develop.

00:31:21.611 --> 00:31:23.505
So, I think that that's

00:31:23.506 --> 00:31:25.785
the thing that we're
talking about here,

00:31:25.786 --> 00:31:28.650
but it's even a grandeur
thing we're talking

00:31:28.651 --> 00:31:31.475
about bringing humans into
it in a very in central way;

00:31:31.476 --> 00:31:33.400
and their utilities,
and their desires and

00:31:33.401 --> 00:31:35.910
the conflicts or the corporation
that's involved there,

00:31:35.911 --> 00:31:38.445
and we're talking
about data and so on.

00:31:38.446 --> 00:31:39.850
So, when you start to do that,

00:31:39.851 --> 00:31:41.840
you realize it's going
to be very much decades.

00:31:41.841 --> 00:31:43.305
It's not going to emerge, AI is

00:31:43.306 --> 00:31:45.100
not going to happen
in five years here,

00:31:45.101 --> 00:31:48.765
and who cares if we get
systems that look like humans.

00:31:48.766 --> 00:31:50.830
It's really about these systems.

00:31:50.831 --> 00:31:52.740
So, in my op-ed, I try to come

00:31:52.741 --> 00:31:54.505
up with very simple examples

00:31:54.506 --> 00:31:56.970
of problems you might

00:31:56.971 --> 00:31:59.540
think are already easily
solved or whatever but aren't.

00:31:59.541 --> 00:32:00.950
So, provenance.

00:32:00.951 --> 00:32:05.940
So, database people are
interested in a piece of data,

00:32:05.941 --> 00:32:08.555
not just the piece of data
itself but where it came from.

00:32:08.556 --> 00:32:09.815
Some metadata around it.

00:32:09.816 --> 00:32:10.920
Who did it?

00:32:10.921 --> 00:32:12.680
When was it done? And all that,

00:32:12.681 --> 00:32:15.005
and that can be used helpfully in

00:32:15.006 --> 00:32:16.940
various database systems
to make sure

00:32:16.941 --> 00:32:19.045
something is coherent and
consistent and so on.

00:32:19.046 --> 00:32:20.670
It's much, much worse when you

00:32:20.671 --> 00:32:22.110
think about learning systems,

00:32:22.111 --> 00:32:24.625
and data analysis, and
predictions and decisions.

00:32:24.626 --> 00:32:27.155
So, I use an example
of medical data that

00:32:27.156 --> 00:32:29.910
at any time you sit down
in front of a doctor,

00:32:29.911 --> 00:32:31.180
they're going to think,

00:32:31.181 --> 00:32:33.140
"Well, you've got
some weird symptoms here.

00:32:33.141 --> 00:32:34.730
Have I ever seen
anything like that?"

00:32:34.731 --> 00:32:37.255
And hopefully, they read
all the medical literature,

00:32:37.256 --> 00:32:39.690
so they know all the data,
but maybe they don't, right?

00:32:39.691 --> 00:32:41.005
But there's always not going

00:32:41.006 --> 00:32:42.430
to be a particularly good match

00:32:42.431 --> 00:32:43.910
between what they see in you and

00:32:43.911 --> 00:32:45.680
your symptoms and others,

00:32:45.681 --> 00:32:46.815
and maybe ideally there's

00:32:46.816 --> 00:32:49.255
a really good match;
but in general,

00:32:49.256 --> 00:32:52.450
what you're doing is you're
taking somewhat ill formed

00:32:52.451 --> 00:32:53.850
ad hoc set of
observations that were

00:32:53.851 --> 00:32:56.115
collected at a different place
in a different time,

00:32:56.116 --> 00:32:57.180
in a different situation,

00:32:57.181 --> 00:32:58.355
in a different context
and you're making

00:32:58.356 --> 00:32:59.575
a judgment about how relevant

00:32:59.576 --> 00:33:01.035
they are to
the current situation.

00:33:01.036 --> 00:33:02.610
It's never going to be the case

00:33:02.611 --> 00:33:04.150
that the current situation
just fits exactly

00:33:04.151 --> 00:33:05.515
the data you collected
yesterday for

00:33:05.516 --> 00:33:07.495
that particular
purpose, all right?

00:33:07.496 --> 00:33:10.150
So, being able to
assess the relevance,

00:33:10.151 --> 00:33:13.150
all that data is
actually irrelevant,

00:33:13.151 --> 00:33:15.255
therefore the provenance of

00:33:15.256 --> 00:33:18.360
that data is not relevant
to the situation,

00:33:18.361 --> 00:33:20.075
therefore I cut
off my neural net.

00:33:20.076 --> 00:33:21.570
I do not let it make
the inference that it

00:33:21.571 --> 00:33:23.625
seems like it wants
to make, right?

00:33:23.626 --> 00:33:24.965
I need to have my arrow bars

00:33:24.966 --> 00:33:26.850
reflect that relevance
calculation.

00:33:26.851 --> 00:33:28.180
Moreover, it's often going to be

00:33:28.181 --> 00:33:29.870
the case that some data was

00:33:29.871 --> 00:33:31.610
gathered at some place
in time and

00:33:31.611 --> 00:33:33.440
it fed through many pathways,

00:33:33.441 --> 00:33:35.420
and it comes in and to
us it looks like a bunch

00:33:35.421 --> 00:33:37.315
of independent evidence,
and we say, "Well,

00:33:37.316 --> 00:33:41.370
I'm seen 5,000 people were
saying this thing and it was

00:33:41.371 --> 00:33:45.995
just that someone created
the rumor back when."

00:33:45.996 --> 00:33:48.090
So, our political world
is full of this,

00:33:48.091 --> 00:33:50.190
our journalistic world is
full of this and a lot of

00:33:50.191 --> 00:33:53.010
false positives are happening.

00:33:53.011 --> 00:33:54.990
People still don't understand

00:33:54.991 --> 00:33:56.560
much about false discovery rate,

00:33:56.561 --> 00:33:57.820
that your goal in
life shouldn't be

00:33:57.821 --> 00:33:59.475
to make accuracy high.

00:33:59.476 --> 00:34:01.560
It should make that relative
to the discoveries.

00:34:01.561 --> 00:34:03.905
There's a denominator
and a numerator there.

00:34:03.906 --> 00:34:06.010
Our current systems that do

00:34:06.011 --> 00:34:07.820
neural nets and all
that are doing very,

00:34:07.821 --> 00:34:09.190
very little with uncertainty.

00:34:09.191 --> 00:34:12.275
They're doing very little
with what if questions.

00:34:12.276 --> 00:34:13.355
They're doing very little

00:34:13.356 --> 00:34:14.810
with the kind of things
you need to have in

00:34:14.811 --> 00:34:16.330
real intelligence system even if

00:34:16.331 --> 00:34:18.155
it's not a super intelligence do.

00:34:18.156 --> 00:34:18.970
In fact, they're doing

00:34:18.971 --> 00:34:21.200
essentially nothing
with uncertainty,

00:34:21.201 --> 00:34:23.665
and so huge gap.

00:34:23.666 --> 00:34:25.195
So, that's kind of

00:34:25.196 --> 00:34:27.085
the engineering discipline
needs to emerge.

00:34:27.086 --> 00:34:28.735
It's not enough
just to know how to

00:34:28.736 --> 00:34:30.160
put together basic ingredients

00:34:30.161 --> 00:34:33.225
to build up a pattern
recognition system.

00:34:33.226 --> 00:34:35.240
If that pattern recognition
system takes in

00:34:35.241 --> 00:34:37.780
the symptoms of me in
the doctor's office and says,

00:34:37.781 --> 00:34:39.435
"You need a heart operation."

00:34:39.436 --> 00:34:42.850
I'm going to say, "What's
your air bar on that?

00:34:42.851 --> 00:34:45.740
Are you sure? Is it
99 percent sure?

00:34:45.741 --> 00:34:47.210
Where is that air bar
coming from?

00:34:47.211 --> 00:34:49.180
Did you know a certain fact
about my family?

00:34:49.181 --> 00:34:51.010
Do you know other facts

00:34:51.011 --> 00:34:53.070
about me? Can we
think about this?

00:34:53.071 --> 00:34:55.645
Can we get some more
data?" And so on.

00:34:55.646 --> 00:34:57.895
Those are the things we
just can't do at all.

00:34:57.896 --> 00:34:59.780
Forget even that we
can't mimic what

00:34:59.781 --> 00:35:03.030
a five year old can do in
language and all that.

00:35:03.031 --> 00:35:06.290
We can't do those competencies
about provenance,

00:35:06.291 --> 00:35:07.590
about multiple decision making,

00:35:07.591 --> 00:35:09.945
about uncertainty,
about and so on.

00:35:09.946 --> 00:35:12.205
I just don't see enough people
talking about this stuff.

00:35:12.206 --> 00:35:13.450
&gt;&gt; So, you're taking

00:35:13.451 --> 00:35:18.415
a very systems level approach
to thinking about

00:35:18.416 --> 00:35:19.775
this interesting new set of

00:35:19.776 --> 00:35:22.095
emerging environments that blend

00:35:22.096 --> 00:35:26.345
human computation and
machine computation.

00:35:26.346 --> 00:35:30.920
What do you think
the right frameworks

00:35:30.921 --> 00:35:32.525
for thinking about that are?

00:35:32.526 --> 00:35:35.620
There's clearly a notion
of abstraction.

00:35:37.010 --> 00:35:39.445
You talked about provenance.

00:35:39.446 --> 00:35:41.240
The fact that we make

00:35:41.241 --> 00:35:43.420
multiple decisions not
isolated decisions.

00:35:43.421 --> 00:35:45.055
The fact that the world is not

00:35:45.056 --> 00:35:47.190
IID by any stretch
of the imagination.

00:35:47.191 --> 00:35:48.410
In fact, that's probably

00:35:48.411 --> 00:35:50.175
the exception rather
than the rule.

00:35:50.176 --> 00:35:54.450
How do you start
thinking from either a

00:35:54.451 --> 00:35:56.285
theoretical or
an engineering perspective

00:35:56.286 --> 00:35:58.540
about providing some
of those tools?

00:35:58.541 --> 00:36:00.510
I know you have a little bit
of work in that area.

00:36:00.511 --> 00:36:02.925
&gt;&gt; Yeah. Well, so,

00:36:02.926 --> 00:36:06.290
I think we're going to need
a new Kolmogorov to emerge,

00:36:06.291 --> 00:36:08.085
or a new Turin or
whatever to really,

00:36:08.086 --> 00:36:09.605
really break open new things;

00:36:09.606 --> 00:36:10.720
and whoever she is,

00:36:10.721 --> 00:36:13.150
it's going to be
great. I can't wait.

00:36:13.270 --> 00:36:16.410
But the best I can do in
my lifetime at least,

00:36:16.411 --> 00:36:17.920
as I alluded to earlier,

00:36:17.921 --> 00:36:19.600
is to not just look
within one discipline.

00:36:19.601 --> 00:36:21.830
Not just think
that one to one technique,

00:36:21.831 --> 00:36:23.420
and one way of thinking
is the right answer.

00:36:23.421 --> 00:36:25.985
So, I do believe that

00:36:25.986 --> 00:36:29.005
the vast scope of
inferential thinking,

00:36:29.006 --> 00:36:31.370
of thinking about what's
out in the real world and

00:36:31.371 --> 00:36:33.450
how to infer that from
the limited data I see,

00:36:33.451 --> 00:36:35.310
including all the
sampling patterns that

00:36:35.311 --> 00:36:37.300
might have gone into with
the biases that might arise.

00:36:37.301 --> 00:36:40.315
Those are ideas that come
for 300 years of statistics.

00:36:40.316 --> 00:36:42.575
It always pains me when
I see people that are

00:36:42.576 --> 00:36:43.710
just or more

00:36:43.711 --> 00:36:44.910
narrowly in the machine
learning world will say,

00:36:44.911 --> 00:36:46.180
"Well, here's a new
problem, we've learned

00:36:46.181 --> 00:36:47.840
about fairness before or bias."

00:36:47.841 --> 00:36:49.820
Well, yes, people have.

00:36:49.821 --> 00:36:52.980
So, if you take the vast scope
of inferential thinking,

00:36:52.981 --> 00:36:55.510
and you put that together
with the slightly as vast,

00:36:55.511 --> 00:36:57.910
but amazing computational era,

00:36:57.911 --> 00:36:59.130
the last hundred years,

00:36:59.131 --> 00:37:04.590
and the signal processing
control theory perspectives.

00:37:04.591 --> 00:37:07.020
There's already some amazing
intellectual challenges

00:37:07.021 --> 00:37:08.180
in trying to bring
those together.

00:37:08.181 --> 00:37:09.645
Once you do, you get real power.

00:37:09.646 --> 00:37:12.265
You can have systems
that can actually say,

00:37:12.266 --> 00:37:15.820
in some sort of amount of
computational resources,

00:37:15.821 --> 00:37:16.810
I can give you answers that are

00:37:16.811 --> 00:37:18.010
trustable in
a computational sense,

00:37:18.011 --> 00:37:20.200
but also I've got [inaudible].

00:37:20.201 --> 00:37:21.310
Now to me, I actually think

00:37:21.311 --> 00:37:24.175
in my last 15 years of my career,

00:37:24.176 --> 00:37:25.900
and my last third let's say,

00:37:25.901 --> 00:37:27.210
I'm going to do my best to bring

00:37:27.211 --> 00:37:29.770
economic principles to bear
with these others as well.

00:37:29.771 --> 00:37:32.560
So, you know, I do
think if you make

00:37:32.561 --> 00:37:35.390
one decision or
a sequeled decision

00:37:35.391 --> 00:37:37.815
that's kind of classical
AI statistics,

00:37:37.816 --> 00:37:40.320
but if you have to
make, your system

00:37:40.321 --> 00:37:42.855
has to make 100,000
decisions simultaneously,

00:37:42.856 --> 00:37:44.350
no human being has to do that,

00:37:44.351 --> 00:37:45.660
so we didn't evolve to do it.

00:37:45.661 --> 00:37:47.585
But our system rigged
out to do that,

00:37:47.586 --> 00:37:49.275
and so you need
a new kind of thinking.

00:37:49.276 --> 00:37:51.630
So, economic thinking is not

00:37:51.631 --> 00:37:53.965
about a single person and
their thinking style.

00:37:53.966 --> 00:37:55.410
It's about large collections not

00:37:55.411 --> 00:37:57.570
just of people, but
of other entities.

00:37:57.571 --> 00:38:00.550
There has been work or
those algorithmic game theory,

00:38:00.551 --> 00:38:01.729
there's econometrics

00:38:01.730 --> 00:38:02.960
where these fields have
been brought together,

00:38:02.961 --> 00:38:05.415
but as usual, in
very limited ways.

00:38:05.416 --> 00:38:09.950
So, I believe if you bring
organizational thinking

00:38:09.951 --> 00:38:14.885
and together with inferential
and computational,

00:38:14.886 --> 00:38:16.360
already the challenge is immense,

00:38:16.361 --> 00:38:17.840
and that's where we need to
face a lot these promises.

00:38:17.841 --> 00:38:19.150
Of course, then, you're not done,

00:38:19.151 --> 00:38:21.360
there's psychological,
and social,

00:38:21.361 --> 00:38:22.850
and ethical, et cetera, etc.

00:38:22.851 --> 00:38:25.030
But still, just this hard core

00:38:25.031 --> 00:38:29.265
of a building systems that
are economically reasonable,

00:38:29.266 --> 00:38:30.760
and the way they set
up incentives among

00:38:30.761 --> 00:38:33.185
multiple components
of the system,

00:38:33.186 --> 00:38:35.560
and that are able to
integrate humans,

00:38:35.561 --> 00:38:38.365
that humans can have
transparency into them.

00:38:38.366 --> 00:38:41.650
That they have statistical
solidity behind them,

00:38:41.651 --> 00:38:43.410
not just in theirs
p-values or error bars,

00:38:43.411 --> 00:38:45.705
but there's been
an appropriate sampling pattern

00:38:45.706 --> 00:38:46.850
or has been a design-

00:38:46.851 --> 00:38:47.230
&gt;&gt; Robust.

00:38:47.231 --> 00:38:48.570
&gt;&gt; There's robust, and then

00:38:48.571 --> 00:38:50.070
that these things
will work over time.

00:38:50.071 --> 00:38:50.175
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:38:50.176 --> 00:38:51.600
&gt;&gt; Like in the provenance
issue doesn't come

00:38:51.601 --> 00:38:53.335
up if you're looking
at one time slice.

00:38:53.336 --> 00:38:53.810
&gt;&gt; That' right.

00:38:53.811 --> 00:38:54.840
&gt;&gt; Right? Provenance comes

00:38:54.841 --> 00:38:56.125
up because the data
is out of date,

00:38:56.126 --> 00:38:57.410
it was collected last year

00:38:57.411 --> 00:38:58.785
in a different city and all that.

00:38:58.786 --> 00:39:04.330
So, just talking all those
gets you both excited,

00:39:04.331 --> 00:39:06.740
but also, I think it's daunting.

00:39:06.741 --> 00:39:09.040
I think we have
a little bit too much hysteria

00:39:09.041 --> 00:39:11.520
about how great everything
is were done.

00:39:11.521 --> 00:39:13.720
I think the younger people
will maybe get this.

00:39:13.721 --> 00:39:16.300
No, this is the beginning
and it's not a gold rush.

00:39:16.301 --> 00:39:18.120
It's the start of
chemical engineering,

00:39:18.121 --> 00:39:21.520
but you know to the power of 10.

00:39:21.521 --> 00:39:23.560
It'll take decades and we engage,

00:39:23.561 --> 00:39:25.095
roll up your sleeves and try to

00:39:25.096 --> 00:39:26.850
make sure that we don't overhype,

00:39:26.851 --> 00:39:28.970
and we don't get people
going crazy about things

00:39:28.971 --> 00:39:31.290
like superhuman intelligence,

00:39:31.291 --> 00:39:32.510
which is not going to happen.

00:39:32.511 --> 00:39:35.830
&gt;&gt; Well, machines and humans
have complimentary skills.

00:39:35.831 --> 00:39:37.210
&gt;&gt; They're complimentary.
That's, right.

00:39:37.211 --> 00:39:39.890
&gt;&gt; I think a simpler level,

00:39:39.891 --> 00:39:42.170
you're talking about
sort of massive systems

00:39:42.171 --> 00:39:43.525
and massive scales and

00:39:43.526 --> 00:39:47.520
hundreds of thousands of
interoperating parts.

00:39:47.521 --> 00:39:48.710
&gt;&gt; But that's the real world

00:39:48.711 --> 00:39:49.980
we're living already right now.

00:39:49.981 --> 00:39:51.900
You think about
transportation systems, right?

00:39:51.901 --> 00:39:53.920
That all these cars
are going around.

00:39:53.921 --> 00:39:56.530
They're on these limited
resources called highways.

00:39:56.531 --> 00:39:59.005
There is danger,
and there's issues.

00:39:59.006 --> 00:40:00.355
So, if you put that into,

00:40:00.356 --> 00:40:02.020
I mean Uber and Didi and

00:40:02.021 --> 00:40:03.670
already do a limited
amount of this.

00:40:03.671 --> 00:40:05.070
But you could easily
create congestion.

00:40:05.071 --> 00:40:06.570
You can create troubles
if you don't kind of

00:40:06.571 --> 00:40:08.220
think about the linked
set of decisions

00:40:08.221 --> 00:40:10.590
that a group of
people have to make

00:40:10.591 --> 00:40:13.330
and the system has to
do that quote, unquote.

00:40:13.331 --> 00:40:16.770
That's could be partly human
and partly a computation.

00:40:16.771 --> 00:40:19.600
&gt;&gt; There's a very
interesting world of I think

00:40:19.601 --> 00:40:22.160
hybrid computation systems
that we don't know

00:40:22.161 --> 00:40:25.750
a lot about the principles of.

00:40:25.751 --> 00:40:28.730
Those systems are now
situated in a real world,

00:40:28.731 --> 00:40:31.320
they're not just in a simulated
world or on a laboratory.

00:40:31.321 --> 00:40:32.890
So, I think, as you point out,

00:40:32.891 --> 00:40:35.035
there are just
immense challenges,

00:40:35.036 --> 00:40:37.155
but also immense opportunities.

00:40:37.156 --> 00:40:40.025
Let me ask you one last question.

00:40:40.026 --> 00:40:43.455
You've had an amazing
set of students

00:40:43.456 --> 00:40:49.615
over the last 20 or 30
years. I wrote some.

00:40:49.616 --> 00:40:51.720
Here's for Ben Geow, David Blye,

00:40:51.721 --> 00:40:53.610
Emily Fox, Zupan Ghahramani,

00:40:53.611 --> 00:40:55.720
Thomas Hoffman, Tomioka Lo,

00:40:55.721 --> 00:40:58.840
Percy Lang, Marina
Mallet, Andrew Aing,

00:40:58.841 --> 00:41:00.835
Stender Singh, Alex Shane,

00:41:00.836 --> 00:41:02.870
more recently we talked to,

00:41:02.871 --> 00:41:04.810
I guess one of
your current students

00:41:04.811 --> 00:41:07.440
Aisha Wilson is
joining the New York,

00:41:07.441 --> 00:41:09.420
New England lab I guess.

00:41:09.421 --> 00:41:11.130
&gt;&gt; Lester Mackey
is already there.

00:41:11.131 --> 00:41:13.440
&gt;&gt; Lester Mackey is
already there, right?

00:41:13.870 --> 00:41:17.590
Peter Bodik is already
here in Redmond.

00:41:17.591 --> 00:41:22.230
What advice do you have
for the young students

00:41:22.231 --> 00:41:26.610
getting involved in computer
science machine learning?

00:41:26.611 --> 00:41:28.490
What should they study?

00:41:28.491 --> 00:41:31.310
How should they get
out of, in many ways,

00:41:31.311 --> 00:41:33.430
the rat race of climbing

00:41:33.431 --> 00:41:36.730
up the leaderboard and
isolated problems?

00:41:37.510 --> 00:41:42.200
&gt;&gt; You know, I don't have
any secrets about it.

00:41:42.201 --> 00:41:45.615
Every year, I'm
amazed by how smart

00:41:45.616 --> 00:41:49.750
these people are and
how enthusiastic,

00:41:49.751 --> 00:41:52.675
entrepreneurial, and
progressive minded they are.

00:41:52.676 --> 00:41:54.480
I get very, very
inspired by that.

00:41:54.481 --> 00:41:56.280
I just try to look
with each person,

00:41:56.281 --> 00:41:58.140
and kind of look at who they
are as an individual and

00:41:58.141 --> 00:42:01.225
what they've got, and
what they're missing.

00:42:01.226 --> 00:42:02.770
Get them to realize that it takes

00:42:02.771 --> 00:42:03.810
five or six years to kind

00:42:03.811 --> 00:42:07.960
of realize what they're
have inside of themselves.

00:42:08.680 --> 00:42:12.270
I tend to encourage them
don't be in a rush.

00:42:12.271 --> 00:42:14.060
Again, this is not a gold rush.

00:42:14.061 --> 00:42:15.320
This is not your opportunity
to make money.

00:42:15.321 --> 00:42:16.590
This is not
your opportunity to be

00:42:16.591 --> 00:42:18.960
famous or whatever is your
opportunity to become a guru.

00:42:18.961 --> 00:42:20.870
Your option is to
study and to actually

00:42:20.871 --> 00:42:23.140
say something
meaningful and deep.

00:42:23.141 --> 00:42:25.510
I'll just say what
everybody else is saying.

00:42:25.511 --> 00:42:27.305
I try to get ahead of everybody.

00:42:27.306 --> 00:42:28.510
But just, head off,

00:42:28.511 --> 00:42:29.730
I often tell them

00:42:29.731 --> 00:42:30.995
how often or I thought
go on one direction.

00:42:30.996 --> 00:42:32.420
You see everybody going that way,

00:42:32.421 --> 00:42:34.400
appreciate why they're
doing it and all that.

00:42:34.401 --> 00:42:36.600
But appreciate also what
they're leaving and missing and

00:42:36.601 --> 00:42:39.125
then go worthwhile and then
you can take your own time.

00:42:39.126 --> 00:42:41.065
You can do it at your own pace.

00:42:41.066 --> 00:42:42.710
You can have a lot
of comfort with

00:42:42.711 --> 00:42:44.495
eventually when you
start to say things.

00:42:44.496 --> 00:42:46.065
So, a lot of those great students

00:42:46.066 --> 00:42:49.470
who really have done
some great things.

00:42:49.471 --> 00:42:52.575
They have published
a small handful of papers.

00:42:52.576 --> 00:42:54.210
Not all of them are
some kind of examples

00:42:54.211 --> 00:42:56.330
who publish way more
than they should have.

00:42:56.331 --> 00:42:58.550
But a few of them published
a small handful of papers,

00:42:58.551 --> 00:42:59.765
but they were deep papers

00:42:59.766 --> 00:43:01.630
and it was a pleasure
to be able to

00:43:01.631 --> 00:43:05.120
write about how they really
solved a big challenge.

00:43:05.121 --> 00:43:08.005
Aisha Wilson, [inaudible] theory

00:43:08.006 --> 00:43:12.315
for continuous dynamical systems
that do optimization.

00:43:12.316 --> 00:43:16.570
Lester Mackey did this amazing
work on Stein's method,

00:43:16.571 --> 00:43:18.230
applied the matrix concentration

00:43:18.231 --> 00:43:19.745
even though it was
not at all his area,

00:43:19.746 --> 00:43:22.675
it was an orthogonal cut in
the space that he was taking.

00:43:22.676 --> 00:43:24.340
He spent a year or
two working on it,

00:43:24.341 --> 00:43:25.690
something beautiful came out.

00:43:25.691 --> 00:43:27.190
A handful of those kind

00:43:27.191 --> 00:43:28.290
of things and people
pay attention.

00:43:28.291 --> 00:43:29.800
This is someone who
could actually think in

00:43:29.801 --> 00:43:31.725
new ways and help
us to move along.

00:43:31.726 --> 00:43:34.465
I guess the other point
I want to make is that I

00:43:34.466 --> 00:43:35.835
loved all my students

00:43:35.836 --> 00:43:37.750
partly because of how
great their minds were,

00:43:37.751 --> 00:43:40.210
but also how friendly and
fun they were to be around.

00:43:40.211 --> 00:43:43.530
So, when I pick students,
it's kind of enthusiasm and

00:43:43.531 --> 00:43:47.045
love of abstraction,
of mathematical ideas,

00:43:47.046 --> 00:43:49.235
of trying understand deeply,

00:43:49.236 --> 00:43:51.735
but also humility about

00:43:51.736 --> 00:43:54.825
each one of our own role
in this and how we,

00:43:54.826 --> 00:43:56.640
being in a social environment

00:43:56.641 --> 00:43:57.730
and connecting to each other.

00:43:57.731 --> 00:44:00.525
It's the best way for all
of us to work and it's fun.

00:44:00.526 --> 00:44:03.830
So, I've had a
particularly social group.

00:44:03.831 --> 00:44:04.990
They've all made
friends with each

00:44:04.991 --> 00:44:07.875
other and made friends
with them too.

00:44:07.876 --> 00:44:10.110
I feel very connected
not just to them,

00:44:10.111 --> 00:44:12.660
but to a lot of other
researchers out in the world.

00:44:12.661 --> 00:44:13.890
I do travel around in Europe,

00:44:13.891 --> 00:44:15.230
in China, and other places a lot,

00:44:15.231 --> 00:44:18.380
and I easily find myself
kind of in grief, you know,

00:44:18.381 --> 00:44:20.430
getting involved
in people's lives

00:44:20.431 --> 00:44:21.810
there and how they think

00:44:21.811 --> 00:44:23.420
and what they do
because I think we

00:44:23.421 --> 00:44:25.350
all are really sharing
a lot of this.

00:44:25.351 --> 00:44:28.450
So, I do very much resist

00:44:28.451 --> 00:44:30.720
the AI is because
she is going to be

00:44:30.721 --> 00:44:31.900
done in this country or that

00:44:31.901 --> 00:44:33.100
country, sort of thing, and all.

00:44:33.101 --> 00:44:34.250
It's really about trade,

00:44:34.251 --> 00:44:35.640
and it's really about shared

00:44:35.641 --> 00:44:37.560
understanding and
shared development.

00:44:37.561 --> 00:44:40.015
I think that the younger
people just get that.

00:44:40.016 --> 00:44:41.870
So, as long as we
kind of encourage

00:44:41.871 --> 00:44:43.930
them that you don't have
to give up on that,

00:44:43.931 --> 00:44:47.760
that you can build a career
that is based on sincerity,

00:44:47.761 --> 00:44:52.100
honesty, and study, and you can.

00:44:52.101 --> 00:44:55.590
That their natural strengths
come right out.

00:44:55.591 --> 00:44:58.315
&gt;&gt; I love the reflections on

00:44:58.316 --> 00:45:02.495
this being giving
people space to grow,

00:45:02.496 --> 00:45:04.080
not feeling like
they have to publish

00:45:04.081 --> 00:45:06.135
10 MIPS papers in a year.

00:45:06.136 --> 00:45:08.740
Doing it as part of
a larger intellectual community.

00:45:08.741 --> 00:45:10.340
When I think back
on the PDP days,

00:45:10.341 --> 00:45:13.500
there was that sense of
energy and excitement.

00:45:13.501 --> 00:45:16.450
I love those reflections.

00:45:16.451 --> 00:45:20.055
For new students in
thinking about how to

00:45:20.056 --> 00:45:23.370
get into these areas
and to make progress.

00:45:23.371 --> 00:45:24.825
Part of it is to slow down,

00:45:24.826 --> 00:45:28.135
take a broader perspective
and surround yourself

00:45:28.136 --> 00:45:31.730
in an environment where
you can share ideas,

00:45:31.731 --> 00:45:33.790
share nascent ideas, and work

00:45:33.791 --> 00:45:36.475
together to come up to
some great solution, so.

00:45:36.476 --> 00:45:39.785
&gt;&gt; Yeah. Microsoft is
a great, I really,

00:45:39.786 --> 00:45:41.330
over the years continue to be

00:45:41.331 --> 00:45:43.240
amazed by how stable it's been,

00:45:43.241 --> 00:45:45.145
as a kind of an electoral
source in the world.

00:45:45.146 --> 00:45:46.270
I see Microsoft as kind of

00:45:46.271 --> 00:45:47.490
returning to the fore right now.

00:45:47.491 --> 00:45:48.880
Just to give a little plug that

00:45:48.881 --> 00:45:51.600
now I've been here three
times this summer.

00:45:51.601 --> 00:45:53.870
I think it's really kind of watch

00:45:53.871 --> 00:45:56.410
the space of IT development
throughout the world,

00:45:56.411 --> 00:45:58.210
and it said there's
a role we can play

00:45:58.211 --> 00:46:00.470
and it's sort of
a almost a senior citizen.

00:46:00.471 --> 00:46:04.810
But bring trust back to how
IT interacts with the world.

00:46:04.811 --> 00:46:06.935
I think that's one of
the big agenda items.

00:46:06.936 --> 00:46:08.470
I think it's being embraced here.

00:46:08.471 --> 00:46:09.750
I'm real pleased about that.

00:46:09.751 --> 00:46:12.790
So, the academic
industry relation is

00:46:12.791 --> 00:46:14.280
very healthy and Microsoft is

00:46:14.281 --> 00:46:16.080
helping lead that
as we go forward.

00:46:16.081 --> 00:46:19.740
&gt;&gt; I mean for us and for
the rest of the industry,

00:46:19.741 --> 00:46:22.930
I think we need to nurture
that kind of students that you

00:46:22.931 --> 00:46:27.260
and others are developing
and do that together.

00:46:27.261 --> 00:46:29.790
There are lots of things
that folks experience in

00:46:29.791 --> 00:46:32.840
industry that they may or
may not in universities.

00:46:32.841 --> 00:46:34.095
So, I think it's
a beautiful synergy.

00:46:34.096 --> 00:46:35.185
&gt;&gt; Indeed. I agree.

00:46:35.186 --> 00:46:37.040
&gt;&gt; Thanks for being
a part of that with us.

00:46:37.041 --> 00:46:38.005
&gt;&gt; All right, thank you.

00:46:38.006 --> 00:46:38.760
&gt;&gt; Okay.

